<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Fujiwara</h1><p class="st-meta">1087 CE – 1189 CE (approx.) · capital Hiraizumi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A family of northern warlords who ruled northeastern Japan for a century from Hiraizumi, whose golden temples rivalled the splendours of Kyoto.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-kiyohira">Fujiwara no Kiyohira</a>, a survivor of the Former Nine Years' and Later Three Years' Wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu</a>, emerged as master of the north after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyohara-clan">Kiyohara</a> family collapsed in 1087. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a> his base, claiming descent from the court Fujiwara through his father.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Three generations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-kiyohira">Kiyohira</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-motohira">Motohira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-hidehira">Hidehira</a>, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu</a> and Dewa as semi-independent lords while acknowledging the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Their wealth came from gold mines, horses and trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, eagle feathers and goods from the far north. They built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuson-ji">Chuson-ji</a> with its gold-leafed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konjikido">Konjikido</a> hall of 1124 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motsu-ji">Motsu-ji</a> with its Pure Land garden.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-hidehira">Hidehira</a> sheltered the fugitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoshitsune">Minamoto no Yoshitsune</a>. After his death his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-yasuhira">Yasuhira</a> killed Yoshitsune under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> in 1189, but Yoritomo invaded anyway, and the family was destroyed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a> is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-fujiwara.jpg" alt="Northern Fujiwara" width="320" height="404"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanhira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1087 CE – 1189 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hiraizumi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fujiwara no Kiyohira, Fujiwara no Motohira, Fujiwara no Hidehira</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Pure Land Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oshu Fujiwara, Hiraizumi Fujiwara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1138&amp;focus=northern-fujiwara" aria-label="Northern Fujiwara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8176 780 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Fujiwara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8496 1036L8441 1168M8496 1036L8531 1157"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0M8531 1157h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8496 1036h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8496 1036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hiraizumi. <a href="/atlas?year=1138&amp;focus=northern-fujiwara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

